  "Number": 15,
  "Question": "Given that \\(-4 \\le x \\le -2\\) and \\(2 \\le y \\le 4,\\) what is the largest possible value of \\[\\dfrac{x + y}{x}?\\]",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\(-1\\)",
    "B": "\\(-\\dfrac{1}{2}\\)",
    "C": "\\(0\\)",
    "D": "\\(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\)",
    "E": "\\(1\\)"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "D",
  "Solution": "Changing to a positive denominator makes the bounds easier to use. Let \\(t=-x\\), so \\(2\\le t\\le4\\). In particular, neither \\(x\\) nor \\(t\\) is zero, and\n\\[\\frac{x+y}{x}=1-\\frac{y}{t}.\\]\nTo maximize this expression, we want to make the positive ratio \\(y/t\\) as small as possible. Since \\(y\\ge2\\) and \\(0<t\\le4\\),\n\\[\\frac{y}{t}\\ge\\frac{2}{t}\\ge\\frac{2}{4}=\\frac12.\\]\nTherefore \\(1-y/t\\le1/2\\). Equality holds when \\(y=2\\) and \\(t=4\\), corresponding to the allowed values \\(x=-4\\) and \\(y=2\\). The largest possible value is \\(\\boxed{\\frac12}\\), choice \\(\\text{D}\\).",
